<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Bowling Tracker"
  description="Case study: a private bowling product for sessions, equipment, oil patterns, drills, leagues, and analytics."
  eyebrow="Sports product · Personal analytics"
  lede="A final score tells me what I shot. It does not tell me why. Bowling Tracker keeps the useful context—ball, lane, pattern, practice, league, adjustments—without turning every trip to the lanes into a data-entry job."
  status="Active development"
  access="Private repository · Public-safe case study"
  tags={['Flutter', 'Offline sync', 'Analytics', 'Domain rules']}
  sections={['The problem', 'Domain decisions that matter', 'Narrow does not mean shallow', 'Why it stays private']}
  tone="slate"
  visual="bowling"
>
  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A score tells you what happened, but not necessarily why. Useful bowling history needs to preserve enough context to compare performance across balls, alleys, patterns, leagues, tournaments, practice sessions, and time—while still allowing fast entry during play.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Domain decisions that matter</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Derived statistics are computed from source events rather than stored beside them and allowed to drift.</li>
      <li>Completed competitive/practice games feed analytics; social sessions can remain in history without contaminating performance metrics.</li>
      <li>Ball attribution lives at the delivery level so equipment analysis has evidence behind it.</li>
      <li>Retired equipment is preserved rather than deleted, because historical sessions still need to explain themselves.</li>
      <li>“House pattern” is treated as a grouping, not invented as a fake oil-pattern entity.</li>
      <li>An offline local store and later synchronization support the real environment. Bowling centers are not exactly famous for enterprise-grade Wi-Fi.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="case-callout">
    <h2>Narrow does not mean shallow</h2>
    <p>
      I do not want a generic sports platform with a bowling skin. Once the session model is sound, drills, leave conversion, ball maintenance, trends, league detail, and equipment analysis can grow naturally from bowling data instead of from a giant feature checklist.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why it stays private</h2>
    <p>
      The repository is not needed to demonstrate the product judgment. A public-safe case study can show the domain model, rules, offline strategy, and analytics philosophy while personal performance records and user-specific data remain private.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
